(1, 47, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'In nephrology patients, hypocalcemia is often seen due to:', '["Low phosphate levels", "Excessive vitamin D production", "High protein intake", "Reduced kidney function"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Impaired kidneys have difficulty converting vitamin D to its active form, affecting', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 48, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A patient in the outpatient clinic has a potassium level of 6.2 mEq/L. What is the most appropriate nursing intervention?', '["Schedule the patient for an immediate dialysis session", "Educate the patient on a potassium-restricted diet", "Administer oral potassium supplements", "Monitor the patient''s heart rhythm for arrhythmias"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is: Monitor the patient''s heart rhythm for arrhythmias.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 49, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Which of the following is not classification of obstructive uropathy?', '["Renal stone", "Acute", "Upper urinary tract obstruction", "Chronic"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The correct answer is: Renal stone.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 50, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Hyponatremia in kidney patients is most commonly caused by:', '["High salt intake", "Low protein intake", "Dehydration", "Fluid overload"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Excess water can dilute sodium levels, leading to hyponatremia.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 51, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Urinary tract infection has the following types', '["Two types", "Four types", "Five types", "Three types"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Four types.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 52, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Which of the following is the second most frequent stone in the body', '["Calcium", "Oxalate", "Uric acid", "Cystine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Oxalate.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 53, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'The primary nephrotic syndrome It is subdivided into the following in approximately order of frequency:', '["Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) only", "Minimal change nephropathy only", "Membranous nephropathy, minimal change nephropathy, focal segmental and hereditary/congenital nephropathy", "Hereditary / congenital nephropathy only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is: Membranous nephropathy, minimal change nephropathy, focal segmental and hereditary/congenital nephropathy.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 54, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'which of the following is not the causes and risk factor of the renal calculi', '["metabolic", "climate", "inflammatory factor", "Diet"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is: inflammatory factor.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 55, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'In kidney disease, hyperkalemia can lead to which of the following symptoms?', '["Hyperactivity", "Bradycardia and muscle weakness", "Hypertension and tachycardia", "Increased urination"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Bradycardia and muscle weakness.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 56, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A nurse is assessing a patient with acute kidney injury (AKI) due to nephrotoxic medication. Which assessment finding would most clearly indicate worsening renal function?', '["Decreased serum creatinine", "Normal electrolyte levels", "Increased urine output", "Elevated blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine levels"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is: Elevated blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine levels.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 57, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A patient receiving nephrotoxic antibiotics has an increased risk of AKI. The nurse monitors which lab value most closely?', '["Hemoglobin", "Serum creatinine", "Serum sodium", "Serum glucose"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Serum creatinine.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 58, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A 24-hour urine collection test is primarily used to:', '["Evaluate renal function and protein excretion", "Assess blood glucose levels", "Check for renal artery stenosis", "Measure serum potassium"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A 24-hour urine test helps evaluate kidney function by assessing total protein and', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 59, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'The dialysis related amyloidosis mostly affect', '["Young men", "People who have dialysis for two years", "Older adult and people who have dialysis for more than three years", "Older adult only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is: Older adult and people who have dialysis for more than three years.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 60, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'The most common type of kidney stone is composed of:', '["Calcium oxalate", "Uric acid", "Cystine", "Struvite"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 61, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A minimum urine output of what value should be maintained during MgSO4 therapy?', '["Urine output is not relevant", ">5ml/hr", ">100ml/hr", ">25ml/hr"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Magnesium is excreted by the kidneys, so poor urine output risks the drug accumulating to toxic levels.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 62, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Coughing during suctioning indicates stimulation of cough reflex centre at the', '["Trachea", "Larynx", "Pharynx", "Carina"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The carina is highly sensitive and its stimulation during suctioning triggers a strong cough reflex.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 63, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'High sodium levels is termed _______', '["Hypernatremia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hyper-sodium syndrome", "Hyper-sodiumaemia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'High sodium is hypernatraemia.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 64, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A painless, firm testicular lump in a young man should be investigated urgently for:', '["Testicular cancer", "Varicocele only", "Epididymitis only", "Hydrocele only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ultrasound and tumour markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) are done. Orchidectomy is via an inguinal approach.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 65, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Normal post-operative urine output for a 70 kg adult is at least about:', '["35 mL per hour", "100 mL per day", "3 mL per hour", "5 mL per hour"]'::jsonb, 0, '0.5 mL/kg/hour multiplied by 70 kg gives 35 mL/hour.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 66, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Urethroplasty is surgery to:', '["Remove the bladder", "Repair a urethral stricture", "Remove the kidney", "Fix the testis in the scrotum"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Catheter care is important after surgery.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 67, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Which electrolyte is considered the principal intracellular cation .', '["Bicarbonate", "Chloride", "Sodium", "Potassium"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Potassium is the principal intracellular cation.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 68, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A patient in PACU suddenly becomes hypotensive with wheeze, rash and facial swelling after IV antibiotic. The first drug is:', '["IV furosemide 40 mg", "IM adrenaline 0.5 mg", "IV morphine 10 mg", "Oral antihistamine only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stop the antibiotic, give oxygen, fluids and call for help.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 69, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A patient in the ICU develops organ injury postoperatively. Which laboratory test result would the nurse expect to be abnormal?', '["Elevated creatinine levels", "Low white blood cell count", "Decreased platelet count", "Increased hemoglobin levels"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Elevated creatinine reflects impaired renal function, the laboratory marker most directly affected by postoperative kidney/organ injury.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 70, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'During thyroid surgery, which structure must be preserved to prevent hypocalcaemia?', '["Submandibular duct", "Carotid artery", "Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Parathyroid glands"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The parathyroid glands sit directly behind/within the thyroid and must be preserved to avoid post-op hypocalcaemia.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 71, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A patient develops a urinary tract infection (UTI) following surgery. What nursing interventions can be implemented to manage a UTI?', '["Encourage the patient to hold their urine to strengthen the bladder", "Ensure proper hygiene and cleanliness when using the bathroom", "Limit the patient''s fluid intake to reduce urinary frequency", "Administer antibiotics as prescribed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Antibiotics targeted at the causative organism treat a diagnosed postoperative urinary tract infection.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 72, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A minimum respiratory rate of what value should be maintained during MgSO4 therapy?', '[">12/min", ">6/min", ">30/min", "No specific value is required"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A respiratory rate falling below this threshold suggests magnesium toxicity is depressing breathing.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 73, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Which combination of medications places a critically ill patient at greatest risk of prerenal AKI due to loss of compensatory glomerular autoregulation?', '["ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs", "Proton pump inhibitors and antihistamines", "Beta blockers and statins", "Calcium channel blockers and insulin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'ACE inhibitors block efferent arteriolar constriction while NSAIDs block afferent arteriolar dilation; combined, they abolish the kidney''s ability to maintain GFR during hypoperfusion, precipitating AKI.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 74, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Tall-tented T waves and widened QRS are seen in:', '["Hyperkalemia", "Hyperphosphatemia", "Hyperglycemia", "Hyponatremia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Tall tented T waves with widened QRS is the classic ECG picture of hyperkalaemia.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 75, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'The most accurate day-to-day measure of fluid balance in a critically ill patient is:', '["Urine colour", "Thirst", "Daily weight", "Skin turgor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Weight change closely tracks fluid gain or loss — about 1kg change equals roughly 1 litre of fluid.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 76, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'The first-line vasopressor recommended in septic shock, according to major guidelines, is:', '["Dopamine", "Vasopressin as first choice", "Phenylephrine as first choice", "Norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Norepinephrine is recommended as the first-line vasopressor in septic shock due to its favourable balance of vasoconstriction with limited chronotropic/arrhythmic effects.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 77, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Urine output in an adult post-operatively should be at least:', '["5 mL/kg/hour", "50 mL per day", "0.1 mL/kg/hour", "0.5 mL/kg/hour"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lower outputs suggest hypovolaemia or renal impairment. Check catheter patency before assuming oliguria.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 78, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH) causes:', '["High potassium with a metabolic acidosis", "Hypovolaemia with polyuria and low urine sodium", "Low sodium with concentrated urine, euvolaemic", "High sodium with very dilute urine and thirst"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Treat with fluid restriction; correct sodium slowly (no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours).', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 79, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'A percutaneous nephrolithotomy patient develops breathlessness after surgery. The nurse should suspect:', '["Constipation from the opioid analgesia given after surgery", "Pleural injury such as pneumothorax or hydrothorax", "Normal recovery", "Urinary tract infection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Upper pole access can injure the pleura.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 80, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II', 'In a patient with hyperkalemia, which ECG change warrants IMMEDIATE intervention?', '["Peaked T waves", "Sinus tachycardia", "ST-segment depression", "First-degree AV block"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Peaked T waves are an early sign of hyperkalemia and may progress to life-threatening arrhythmias.', 'NNP 421 · Medical-Surgical Nephrology II'),
(1, 81, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Principles of Advocacy involve the following except:', '["Accountability and susceptibility", "Confidentiality and person Centred Approach", "Clarity of purpose and independence", "Empowerment and equal opportunity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is: Clarity of purpose and independence.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 82, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Consent of gate-keepers is important before conducting any community function. Primarily, this is to:', '["Enhance susceptibility of the people", "Encourage acceptability of the programme", "Encourage advocacy of the programme", "Instill fear among people, in order to facilitate acceptability"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The correct answer is: Enhance susceptibility of the people.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 83, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Avoiding predisposing factors that result to health problems is part of the following levels of prevention?', '["Secondary", "Primordial", "Primary", "Quantinery"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is: Quantinery.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 84, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Occupational exposure to lead and cadmium can cause:', '["Kidney stones only", "Chronic kidney damage", "Glomerulonephritis only", "Polycystic kidneys"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Use protective equipment at work.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 85, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'A community kidney screening outreach should include:', '["Only distributing drugs", "Kidney biopsy for everyone", "Consent, BP, urine dipstick, BMI, education and referral pathways", "Dialysis in the community hall"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ensure follow-up for positives.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 86, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Hepatitis B vaccination protects dialysis patients from:', '["Hypertension", "Kidney stones", "Anaemia", "Hepatitis B infection in dialysis units"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Check antibody levels after the course.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 87, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'CKD stage 3a is defined by an eGFR of:', '["Below 15", "45–59 mL/min/1.73 m²", "90 or above", "15–29"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stage 3b is 30–44.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 88, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Secondary prevention of diabetic kidney disease in a patient with albuminuria includes:', '["Increasing salt intake", "Regular NSAIDs", "Stopping all medicines", "ACE inhibitor or ARB, glucose control and SGLT2 inhibitors where suitable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'These reduce progression.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 89, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Obesity increases kidney disease risk because it:', '["Lowers blood pressure", "Raises the risk of diabetes and hypertension and causes hyperfiltration", "Protects the kidneys", "Has no link"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Weight loss reduces proteinuria.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 90, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Risk factors for CKD include diabetes, hypertension, obesity, smoking and:', '["A family history of kidney disease", "Regular exercise", "Normal blood pressure", "Drinking enough water"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Screen high-risk groups.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 91, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Safe use of herbal remedies should be promoted because some herbs:', '["Contain toxins such as aristolochic acid that damage kidneys", "Have no side effects", "Are tested like modern drugs", "Always improve kidney function"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ask patients about all remedies they use.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 92, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'SGLT2 inhibitors (e.g. dapagliflozin) are used in CKD because they:', '["Cause rapid weight gain", "Raise blood pressure", "Slow kidney function decline and reduce cardiovascular events", "Increase proteinuria"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Watch for genital infections and volume depletion.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 93, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Controlling schistosomiasis in endemic areas helps prevent:', '["Polycystic disease", "Hypertension", "Bladder damage and obstructive uropathy", "Diabetes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Avoid contact with infested water.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 94, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Prevention of contrast nephropathy includes:', '["Restricting fluids", "Checking kidney function first and hydrating at-risk patients", "Giving NSAIDs", "Giving contrast to everyone without checks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Use the lowest contrast dose.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 95, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Tertiary prevention in nephrology includes:', '["Immunisation of children", "Health education in schools", "Dialysis, transplant and rehabilitation to limit complications", "Screening healthy adults"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It reduces disability from established disease.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 96, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Screening for kidney disease is most cost-effective in:', '["Newborns only", "High-risk groups such as people with diabetes or hypertension", "Healthy young adults only", "No one"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Targeted screening finds more cases.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 97, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Monitoring renal function in patients on nephrotoxic drugs like aminoglycosides is an example of:', '["Secondary prevention (early detection)", "Palliation", "Primary prevention", "Rehabilitation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It detects damage early.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 98, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'A national renal registry helps preventive nephrology by:', '["Selling organs", "Treating patients directly", "Replacing dialysis", "Providing data on disease burden to plan services"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Data guide policy and resources.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 99, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Lifestyle advice for a patient with early CKD includes:', '["Drink alcohol daily", "Reduce salt, stop smoking, exercise and keep a healthy weight", "Eat more salt", "Stop all exercise"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Salt below 5 g/day is advised.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(1, 100, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Morning (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology', 'Primary prevention of CKD includes:', '["Dialysis for ESKD", "Kidney transplantation", "Health education, healthy diet, exercise and avoiding nephrotoxins", "Treating hyperkalaemia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It stops disease before it starts.', 'NNP 426 · Preventive Nephrology'),
(2, 1, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Uterine prolapse where the cervix comes out through the vaginal opening is described as:', '["Cystocele only, with the bladder bulging into the vagina", "Normal finding after birth", "Third-degree prolapse (procidentia if complete)", "First-degree prolapse"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Treatments include pessaries or vaginal hysterectomy.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 2, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'The commonest operation for a ruptured tubal pregnancy is:', '["Total hysterectomy", "Salpingectomy", "Salpingostomy", "Salpingo-oophorectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A ruptured tubal pregnancy with haemorrhage is usually treated by salpingectomy (removing the tube).', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 3, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Post-menopausal bleeding should be investigated first to exclude:', '["Endometrial cancer", "Ovarian torsion", "Pregnancy", "Urinary tract infection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy are used.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 4, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'The most common cause of primary postpartum haemorrhage is:', '["Genital tract trauma only", "Retained placenta only", "Coagulation disorders", "Uterine atony"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ''4 Ts'': Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin. Atony is treated with uterine massage, oxytocics, bimanual compression and surgery if needed.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 5, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'In pregnancy plasma volume increases from......... To ...... by 32 weeks'' gestation', '["3.4 to 4.2L", "2.6 to 3.8L", "4 to 5L", "3.2 to 4L"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Plasma volume rises roughly from 2.6L to 3.8L by 32 weeks gestation.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 6, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Fundal height is otherwise called:', '["McDonald''s rule", "McGregor''s rule", "McBannas''s rule", "McBanas''s rule"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The correct answer is: McDonald''s rule.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 7, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Which conflict resolution style involves each party giving up something to reach a middle-ground solution?', '["Collaborating", "Compromising", "Avoiding", "Competing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Compromising is a ''split the difference'' approach where both sides get some, but not all, of what they originally wanted.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 8, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A pregnant patient with severe hyperemesis gravidarum is at risk of thiamine deficiency, which can cause:', '["Wernicke''s encephalopathy", "Only mild fatigue with no other risk", "No neurological consequences", "Improved neurological function"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Prolonged vomiting in hyperemesis gravidarum can deplete thiamine stores, risking Wernicke''s encephalopathy — thiamine replacement is important, especially before glucose administration.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 9, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Before caesarean section, the theatre nurse ensures the patient is positioned:', '["Flat supine without any tilt", "Prone with pillows under the hips", "Right lateral position on the table", "Supine with a left lateral tilt"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Left tilt reduces aortocaval compression by the gravid uterus, preventing supine hypotension and fetal distress.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 10, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Which may be life-saving in managing severe pre-eclampsia?', '["Paracetamol for frontal headache in moderate disease", "Outpatient care when hyperreflexia appears", "Conservative management of imminent eclampsia at 29 weeks", "Delivery, even at a very early gestational age"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Delivery is the definitive cure for pre-eclampsia. In severe disease it may be needed even at an early gestation to save the mother.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 11, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Cervical cerclage is usually inserted at:', '["28–30 weeks of gestation", "4–6 weeks of gestation", "12–14 weeks of gestation", "36–38 weeks of gestation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is removed at about 37 weeks or if labour starts.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 12, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Before caesarean section under general anaesthesia, the patient is given an antacid such as sodium citrate to:', '["Prevent post-operative pain", "Raise blood sugar and give energy before the operation starts", "Increase uterine contractions", "Reduce the acidity of stomach contents in case of aspiration"]'::jsonb, 3, 'H₂ blockers or PPIs are also given.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 13, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'The isthmus of the uterus measures about:', '["7 mm", "7 m", "7 cm", "70 mm"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The isthmus is the narrow junction between the uterine body and cervix, about 7 mm long. It becomes the lower uterine segment in pregnancy.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 14, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A woman in the third trimester with sudden breathlessness, chest pain and tachycardia most likely has:', '["Heartburn", "Pulmonary embolism", "Normal pregnancy breathlessness", "Mild asthma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pregnancy is a hypercoagulable state; LMWH is the treatment.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 15, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Fundal height is used to estimate gestational age in which each centimeter (cm) is equivalent to:', '["1 day", "72 hours", "1 week", "1 month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correct answer is: 1 week.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 16, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A ruptured ectopic pregnancy with shock needs:', '["Methotrexate and discharge", "Waiting for repeat hCG", "Resuscitation and immediate laparotomy or laparoscopy", "Bed rest only, with serial hCG measurements every 48 hours"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cross-match blood and inform theatre urgently.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 17, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'This describe a condition where the fertilized egg implants itself in an area outside of the uterus.', '["Ectopic pregnancy", "Hydatidiform mole", "Molar pregnancy", "Premolar pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A fertilised egg implanting outside the uterus is an ectopic pregnancy.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 18, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'The most common site of endometriosis is the:', '["Lungs and pleura, causing cough during periods", "Ovaries and pelvic peritoneum", "Skin of the arms", "Brain"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It causes painful periods, pain on intercourse and infertility.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 19, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Ovarian cystectomy differs from oophorectomy because it:', '["Removes the cyst and keeps the ovary", "Removes the uterus and both fallopian tubes", "Removes the whole ovary", "Removes both tubes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is preferred in young women to preserve fertility.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 20, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Peripartum cardiomyopathy presents with:', '["Sudden tearing chest pain radiating to the back", "Hypertension and seizures without heart failure", "Fever with a widespread rash and joint pains", "Heart failure in late pregnancy or months after"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is relatively common in northern Nigeria.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 21, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'The incubation period of Ebola virus disease is:', '["1 year", "2–21 days", "2 months", "1–2 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Contacts are followed up for 21 days.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 22, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'This describe the premature separation of a normally situated placenta occurring after the 28th week of pregnancy.', '["Placenta circumventer", "Abruption placentae", "Placenta praevia", "Placenta accrete"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Premature separation of a normally implanted placenta after 28 weeks is abruptio placentae.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 23, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Severe malaria in pregnancy is treated with:', '["Oral metronidazole", "Oral chloroquine only", "IV artesunate", "IM penicillin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pregnant women are at high risk of hypoglycaemia and pulmonary oedema.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 24, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Which statement about pre-eclampsia is correct?', '["Oedema is required for diagnosis", "MgSO4 is used to lower blood pressure in emergencies", "It is mainly a disease of primigravidae", "BP >140/110 defines severe disease"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pre-eclampsia is mainly a disease of first pregnancies (primigravidae). Oedema is no longer diagnostic; MgSO4 prevents fits but does not treat BP.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 25, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'HELLP syndrome is:', '["A form of gestational hypertension only", "An inherited maternal coagulopathy", "A type of fetal growth restriction", "A complication of severe pre-eclampsia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'HELLP (Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets) is a severe complication of pre-eclampsia.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 26, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Which of the following describes a partial praevia?', '["Cervical opening is partially covered by the placenta", "Cervical opening is completely covered by the placenta", "Acute on Chronic praevia", "Placenta extends just to the edge of the cervix"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Partial praevia means the placenta partially (not completely) covers the cervical os.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 27, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A patient on magnesium sulfate after caesarean section for pre-eclampsia should have hourly:', '["Temperature only, as other observations are not affected", "Blood glucose only", "Weight measurements", "Respiratory rate, reflexes and urine output checks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Magnesium is excreted by the kidneys.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 28, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A gravida 9 presents with history of amenorrhea for 7 weeks, PV bleeding, lower abdominal pain, and fainting twice yesterday. Her diagnosis is likely:', '["Abortion", "Implantation bleeding", "Ectopic pregnancy", "Placenta Previa"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amenorrhoea, bleeding, abdominal pain, and fainting in a multigravida strongly suggest ectopic pregnancy.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 29, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'Total abdominal hysterectomy with bilateral salpingo-oophorectomy (TAH-BSO) in a premenopausal woman will cause:', '["No hormonal change", "Weight loss", "Surgical menopause", "Increased fertility"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hormone replacement may be discussed.', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 30, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology', 'A 25 years old para 4, was seen in the clinic with the diagnosis of mastitis. what feeding advice will nurse Iyabo give?', '["Continue breastfeeding", "Wean the baby", "Give infant formula", "Feed the baby with express breast milk"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mastitis does not require stopping breastfeeding — continuing feeding is recommended (helps clear the blockage/infection).', 'NNP 422 · Gynaecology and Obstetrics in Nephrology'),
(2, 31, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The defibrillation energy for a child in VF is:', '["0.4 J/kg", "40 J/kg", "4 J/kg", "360 J for all ages"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Then resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 32, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Newborn resuscitation begins with:', '["Five inflation breaths of 2–3 seconds each", "Deep suction of the stomach for one minute", "Immediate chest compressions at 120/min", "IV adrenaline before any breaths"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inflation breaths open fluid-filled lungs. Reassess heart rate and chest movement.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 33, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'First-line drug for neonatal seizures is:', '["Phenobarbital", "Carbamazepine", "Valproate", "Phenytoin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Loading dose 20 mg/kg IV.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 34, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The estimated weight of a 5-year-old child using (age + 4) × 2 is:', '["30 kg", "10 kg", "25 kg", "18 kg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Use it for drug and fluid doses when the child cannot be weighed.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 35, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The normal blood glucose of a newborn should be maintained above:', '["1.0 mmol/L (about 18 mg/dL)", "2.6 mmol/L (about 47 mg/dL)", "0.5 mmol/L (about 9 mg/dL)", "10 mmol/L (about 180 mg/dL)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Hypoglycaemia can cause brain injury; early feeding and IV dextrose are used.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 36, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The main indication for exchange blood transfusion in a newborn is:', '["Neonatal hypoglycaemia not responding to feeds", "Mild physiological jaundice on day 3", "Uncomplicated prematurity below 34 weeks", "Severe jaundice despite intensive phototherapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It removes bilirubin and sensitised red cells (e.g. in Rh or ABO haemolytic disease) and corrects anaemia, preventing kernicterus.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 37, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A nurse is providing family-centered NICU care. The primary goal of family-centered care is to:', '["Allow the parents to make all of the medical decisions", "Only communicate with the parents when they ask a question", "Involve the parents as partners in the newborn''s care", "Keep the parents separated from the newborn to prevent infection"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Family-centered care recognizes that the family is the constant in the newborn''s life. The goal is to involve the parents as partners in the newborn''s care, empowering them and providing them with the information and skills they need to care for their newborn after discharge.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 38, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Before paediatric surgery, the nurse weighs the child mainly to:', '["Decide the theatre list order", "Calculate drug doses and fluids accurately", "Record growth for school", "Check for obesity only, as weight does not affect doses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Weight in kilograms must be recorded.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 39, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A child weighing 18 kg is prescribed an antibiotic at 10 mg/kg/day divided every 8 hours. How many milligrams should be given per dose?', '["30 mg", "180 mg", "60 mg", "90 mg"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Total daily dose = 18 kg x 10 mg/kg = 180 mg/day. Divided every 8 hours = 3 doses/day, so 180/3 = 60 mg/dose.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 40, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Unexplained fractures in an infant of different ages on X-ray should raise concern of:', '["Non-accidental injury (child abuse)", "Simple falls only", "Vitamin C excess", "Normal growth and development in infancy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Follow safeguarding procedures.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 41, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Respiratory distress syndrome in preterm babies is caused by:', '["Meconium aspiration", "Surfactant deficiency", "Excess lung fluid at term", "Pneumothorax only"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Antenatal steroids and surfactant therapy reduce severity.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 42, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'In a child with suspected appendicitis, the ICU nurse should anticipate perforation if:', '["The child becomes hungry and asks for food", "There is mild nausea only, without vomiting", "The pain moves from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa", "Pain becomes generalised with high fever and rigidity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Young children perforate early because the omentum is less developed.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 43, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Bilious vomiting in a newborn requires an urgent:', '["Upper GI contrast study to exclude malrotation", "A course of oral antibiotics and review", "Stool test for reducing sugars and pH", "Trial of a change of formula milk for 24 hours"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Volvulus can cause gangrene of the whole midgut within hours.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 44, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis typically causes which blood picture?', '["Normal electrolytes always", "Metabolic acidosis with high potassium", "Respiratory acidosis with a low bicarbonate level", "Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correct before surgery.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 45, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Children lose heat quickly during surgery mainly because they have:', '["Higher blood pressure", "A large surface area relative to body weight", "More body fat than adults", "A slower metabolism and more body fat than adults"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Use warm theatres, radiant heaters and warm fluids.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 46, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'In neonatal resuscitation, after drying and stimulating, the next assessment is:', '["Blood glucose level", "Core temperature only", "Non-invasive blood pressure", "Breathing and heart rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If apnoeic or HR below 100, start inflation breaths and ventilation.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 47, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'For a 15 kg child, the maintenance fluid by the Holliday–Segar method is:', '["1500 mL per 24 hours", "1250 mL per 24 hours", "2000 mL per 24 hours", "750 mL per 24 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, '100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg (1000 mL) + 50 mL/kg for the next 5 kg (250 mL).', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 48, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'This condition describe a birth defect where some nerve cells are missing in the large intestine:', '["Hirsh sprung disease", "Appendicitis", "Birth stenosis", "Pyloric Hirsh sprung"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Absent nerve cells (ganglion cells) in the large intestine defines Hirschsprung''s disease.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 49, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'When investigating a newborn with hyperbilirubinaemia, which bilirubin fractions should be measured?', '["Total and unconjugated bilirubin", "Conjugated bilirubin only", "Total bilirubin only", "Total and conjugated bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Measure total AND conjugated (direct) bilirubin. A raised conjugated fraction points to cholestasis or biliary atresia and changes management.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 50, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Fluid maintenance for a 10 kg child using the Holliday-Segar method is:', '["500 mL per 24 hours", "1000 mL per 24 hours", "1500 mL per 24 hours", "2000 mL per 24 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, '100 mL/kg for the first 10 kg, 50 mL/kg for the next 10 kg, and 20 mL/kg for each kg above 20.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 51, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Bacterial meningitis in a child: the priority after suspecting it is:', '["Lumbar puncture before any treatment, even if shocked", "CT scan before any antibiotics in all cases", "Oral antibiotics and observation", "Blood cultures, then IV antibiotics without delay"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Antibiotics must not be delayed by investigations; LP is deferred if ICP is raised or the child is unstable.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 52, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A child with empyema thoracis may need:', '["Chest drain insertion or thoracoscopic surgery", "Only oral fluids", "No treatment, as empyema always clears with time", "Immediate tracheostomy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Antibiotics are given as well.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 53, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A nurse is assessing a newborn for signs of birth asphyxia. Which of the following is a key sign?', '["A pink color and a strong cry", "Poor muscle tone and a low Apgar score", "A vigorous kick and a respiratory rate of 40 breaths per minute", "A heart rate of 120 beats per minute"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Birth asphyxia is a condition where a newborn experiences a lack of oxygen. Key signs include poor muscle tone, a weak or absent cry, and a low Apgar score (less than 7 at 5 minutes), all of which indicate poor respiratory and neurological status.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 54, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'In children, inhaled foreign bodies most often lodge in the:', '["Left main bronchus", "Right main bronchus", "Oesophagus behind the larynx", "Nasopharynx"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The right bronchus is wider and more vertical.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 55, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Which of the following statements regarding the effects of hyperemesis gravidarum on the fetus is FALSE?', '["Normal nausea and vomiting appear to be protective in the first trimester.", "Infants born to women with hyperemesis gravidarum tend to have significantly lower birth weights", "Hip dysplasia has been linked to hyperemesis gravidarum.", "There is evidence that hyperemesis gravidarum is associated with high infant birth weight."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hyperemesis gravidarum is actually linked to LOWER (not higher) infant birth weight, making that statement false.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 56, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A neonate is an infant in the first:', '["30 days of life", "38 days of life", "28 days of life", "20 days of life"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The neonatal period is the first 28 days of life.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 57, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The recommended maintenance fluid for the first 10 kg of body weight (Holliday-Segar) is:', '["0.5 mL/kg/hour", "1 mL/kg/hour", "10 mL/kg/hour", "4 mL/kg/hour"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Next 10 kg: 2 mL/kg/h; each kg above 20: 1 mL/kg/h.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 58, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'An inhaled peanut in a child is removed by:', '["Oral antibiotics and chest physiotherapy at home", "Rigid bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia", "Making the child vomit", "Chest physiotherapy alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Peanuts swell and cause chemical irritation.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 59, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Duodenal atresia is commonly associated with:', '["Cystic fibrosis only", "Down syndrome", "Marfan syndrome", "Turner syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Check for cardiac defects.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 60, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'A premature neonate is at risk of retinopathy of prematurity mainly because of:', '["High or fluctuating oxygen levels", "Feeding with expressed breast milk", "Phototherapy for neonatal jaundice", "Repeated low blood glucose levels"]'::jsonb, 0, 'SpO₂ is targeted (e.g. 90–95%) to avoid hyperoxia.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 61, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'What is the correct sequence of steps for neonatal resuscitation (NALS)?', '["Oxygen, Chest Compressions, Intubation, Medications", "Ventilation, Chest Compressions, Suctioning, Warmth", "Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Drugs", "Warmth, Position, Suction, Dry, Stimulate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The initial steps of neonatal resuscitation, known as the "A-B-C" of the newborn, focus on providing a supportive environment and ensuring a patent airway. The sequence is to provide warmth, position the airway, suction secretions if needed, dry the newborn, and provide tactile stimulation.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 62, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'One of the following neonates does not need resuscitation', '["Baby with congenital heart disease", "Baby whose mother is on sedatives", "Baby with hypothermia", "Baby with heart rate of 100bpm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A heart rate of 100bpm is within the normal newborn range and does not indicate a need for resuscitation.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 63, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The most widely used effective treatment for neonatal jaundice is:', '["Blood transfusion", "Liver transplant", "Phototherapy", "Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Phototherapy is the most widely used, effective treatment for neonatal jaundice. Exchange transfusion is kept for severe or rapidly rising levels.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 64, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Projectile non-bilious vomiting in a 5-week-old boy with an ''olive'' mass in the epigastrium suggests:', '["Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis", "Ileocolic intussusception", "Hirschsprung''s disease of the colon", "Duodenal atresia with stenosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It causes hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis. Fluids and electrolytes are corrected before Ramstedt''s pyloromyotomy.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 65, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Antenatal corticosteroids before preterm delivery are given mainly to:', '["Mature the fetal lungs and reduce RDS", "Treat maternal hypertension in pre-eclampsia", "Prevent maternal infection after delivery", "Stop uterine contractions in preterm labour"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Betamethasone or dexamethasone is given 24 hours to 7 days before delivery.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 66, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) risk is reduced by which key recommendation?', '["Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach)", "Using soft, loose bedding in the crib", "Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)", "Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ''back to sleep'' recommendation — placing infants supine — is a key evidence-based measure shown to reduce SIDS risk.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 67, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The compression depth recommended for an infant during CPR is approximately:', '["8cm", "1cm", "4cm", "5–6cm"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An infant''s chest is much smaller and more fragile, so the target compression depth is shallower, at about 4cm, compared to 5–6cm in an adult.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 68, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Appendicitis in young children is more dangerous than in adults because:', '["It never causes pain in children, so it is found late", "It perforates earlier and is harder to diagnose", "Children cannot have appendicitis", "It always resolves alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The omentum is underdeveloped and less able to wall it off.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 69, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'The narrowest part of the airway in young children (traditionally) is the:', '["Cricoid ring", "Nasopharynx", "Carina", "Vocal cords in all ages"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is why uncuffed tubes were used traditionally.', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 70, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology', 'Imperforate anus (anorectal malformation) is identified at birth by:', '["Bilious vomiting starting at about 6 weeks of age", "Absence of a normal anal opening on inspection", "Projectile vomiting after feeds in the first week", "Frothy saliva and choking at the first feed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Associated anomalies form the VACTERL association (vertebral, anorectal, cardiac, tracheo-oesophageal, renal, limb).', 'NNP 423 · Paediatric Nephrology'),
(2, 71, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Intra-operative nursing diagnosis ''Risk for perioperative positioning injury'' requires interventions such as:', '["Leaving the patient''s limbs unsupported on the table", "Letting skin rest directly on metal parts of the table", "Padding bony prominences and keeping body alignment", "Restraining the patient tightly with straps over joints"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nerve and pressure injuries are prevented by careful positioning.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 72, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'While the nurse is providing a patient personal hygiene, she observes that his skin is excessively dry. During the procedure, he tells her that he is very thirsty. An appropriate nursing diagnosis would be:', '["Impaired skin integrity related to altered circulation", "Potential for impaired skin integrity related to altered gland function", "Impaired skin integrity related to dehydration", "Potential for impaired skin integrity related to dehydration"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dry skin and thirst point to a fluid volume deficit (dehydration) as the underlying cause.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 73, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Following successful treatment of a stressor reaction, a nurse focuses on rehabilitation and helping the client readapt to promote stability and prevent future reoccurrence of the stressor. This reflects which of Neuman''s prevention levels?', '["Primary prevention", "Secondary prevention", "Tertiary prevention", "Reconstitutive prevention only, unrelated to Neuman''s model"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Tertiary prevention occurs after secondary prevention/treatment and focuses on readaptation, reconstitution, and maintaining stability to prevent further regression or recurrence of the stressor reaction.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 74, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Pharmaceutical waste (Drugs) is classified as ;', '["Radioactive waste", "Infectious waste", "Domestic waste", "Toxic waste"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The correct answer is: Toxic waste.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 75, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The assessment approach in which the cardiovascular system is assessed before the genito-urinary system is the:', '["Systematic approach", "Approach by order of priority", "Body systems approach", "Cephalocaudal approach"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The body systems approach assesses one system at a time, e.g. cardiovascular before genito-urinary.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 76, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Which is necessary for making a nursing diagnosis?', '["Designing the care plan", "Performing activities", "Clustering of cues", "Validating the plan"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Grouping related assessment cues (clustering) shows the patterns that support a nursing diagnosis.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 77, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'One of this statement is NOT TRUE about Genotoxic waste', '["They are harmful to human genome", "Absorbent paper waste", "Drugs used for cancer treatment are in this class", "They are cytotoxic"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Absorbent paper waste.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 78, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The nurse is assessing a postoperative adult patient. Which of the following should the nurse document as subjective data?', '["Vital signs", "Laboratory test result", "Patient’s description of pain", "Electrocardiographic (ECG) waveforms"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Subjective data are what the patient reports, such as pain. Vital signs, ECGs and lab results are objective.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 79, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Orem identified three types of nursing systems. One of them is:', '["Individualised client care", "Primary nursing", "Team nursing", "Wholly compensatory"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Orem''s three nursing systems are wholly compensatory, partly compensatory and supportive-educative.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 80, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'COLDSPA pain assessment falls under which functional health pattern?', '["Cognition and perception", "Sleep and rest", "Self-perception and self-concept", "Activity and exercise"]'::jsonb, 0, 'COLDSPA (Character, Onset, Location, Duration, Severity, Pattern, Associated factors) assesses pain, part of Gordon''s cognitive–perceptual pattern.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 81, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A family caregiver of a chronically critically ill ICU patient reports exhaustion, feeling overwhelmed by care responsibilities, and increasing strain in the relationship with the patient. The most appropriate NANDA-I diagnosis is:', '["Interrupted Family Processes", "Ineffective Family Coping", "Caregiver Role Strain", "Compromised Family Coping"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Caregiver Role Strain specifically describes difficulty in performing the family caregiver role, evidenced by exhaustion, feeling overwhelmed, and relational strain.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 82, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Neuman describes three levels of prevention as intervention. Which level is aimed at strengthening the flexible line of defense BEFORE a stressor invades the client system?', '["Secondary prevention", "Primary prevention", "Tertiary prevention", "Quaternary prevention"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary prevention in Neuman''s model occurs before a stressor has invaded the system, aiming to reduce the possibility of encountering the stressor or to strengthen the flexible line of defense, such as through immunization or health teaching.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 83, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Which of the following nursing models draws most directly and explicitly on general systems theory to describe the client as a system interacting with environmental stressors?', '["Henderson''s 14 Basic Needs", "Gordon''s Functional Health Patterns", "Nightingale''s Environmental Theory", "Neuman''s Systems Model"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Betty Neuman explicitly built her Systems Model on general systems theory, portraying the client as an open system with a core structure, lines of defense/resistance, and stressors from the environment.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 84, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A client who was previously the primary breadwinner expresses distress about being unable to return to work after a spinal cord injury and struggles with the loss of that role within the family. This is best classified under which Roy adaptive mode?', '["Self-concept/group identity mode", "Role function mode", "Interdependence mode", "Physiological-physical mode"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The role function mode addresses the roles a person occupies in society (e.g., worker, parent) and how the person adapts to changes in those roles, such as loss of the breadwinner role after injury.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 85, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'All of these are tools used for measurement of quality assurance EXCEPT', '["Peer review", "Boot marking", "Audit", "Clinical pathway"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Boot marking.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 86, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'All of the following are Effects of poor quality of health on the patient EXCEPT', '["Satisfaction with care", "Loss of working hours", "Increased expenses", "Increased length of stay"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The correct answer is: Satisfaction with care.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 87, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The nursing diagnosis ''Acute pain related to surgical incision'' is an example of:', '["A medical diagnosis", "A wellness nursing diagnosis", "An actual nursing diagnosis", "A risk nursing diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Risk diagnoses describe potential problems, e.g. ''Risk for infection''.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 88, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A nurse made Mr Kay''s care plan on admission on Tuesday, then made another care plan for him on Wednesday''s shift. Wednesday''s plan is a(n):', '["Discharge plan", "Initial plan", "Ongoing plan", "Reviewed plan"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The initial plan is made on admission. Plans updated on later shifts as the patient''s status changes are ongoing plans.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 89, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Which is a correctly written nursing diagnosis?', '["Risk for infection related to surgical incision", "Needs IV antibiotics and analgesia every 8 hours", "Acute appendicitis requiring appendicectomy", "Patient is difficult and refuses to cooperate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A nursing diagnosis describes the patient''s response to health problems that nurses can treat independently.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 90, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Which of the following best describes the ''regulator'' coping subsystem in Roy''s model?', '["A subsystem used only for spiritual coping", "A subsystem that processes information through cognitive-emotive channels such as perception and learning", "A subsystem that responds automatically through neural, chemical, and endocrine processes", "A subsystem exclusive to the role-function adaptive mode"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The regulator subsystem is the automatic coping process involving neural, chemical, and endocrine responses, such as the physiological stress response.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 91, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A client reports difficulty falling asleep, frequent nighttime awakenings, and feeling unrested in the morning. Which Gordon functional health pattern is being assessed?', '["Sleep-rest pattern", "Cognitive-perceptual pattern", "Value-belief pattern", "Coping-stress tolerance pattern"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sleep-rest pattern addresses the client''s pattern of sleep, rest, and relaxation, including any sleep disturbances and the client''s perception of adequate rest.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 92, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The nursing diagnosis ''Anxiety related to unknown surgical outcome'' is best managed by:', '["Giving sedatives only, without any explanation", "Telling the patient firmly that there is nothing to fear", "Ignoring the patient so that they calm down alone", "Giving information, answering questions and reassuring"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Explaining what will happen reduces anxiety.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 93, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A client develops an autoimmune reaction that attacks her own joint tissue. Using Neuman''s classification, this stressor would be categorized as:', '["Interpersonal", "Suprapersonal", "Intrapersonal", "Extrapersonal"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An intrapersonal stressor arises from within the individual, such as an internal physiological or emotional process; an autoimmune reaction occurring within the client''s own body fits this category.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 94, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A Nurse at HMC asked a patient to verbalise if he understood the information given during nursing care to the client according to a devised nursing care plan. During which step of the nursing process does such action take place?', '["Implementation", "Planning", "Assessment", "Evaluation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Checking whether the patient understood and achieved the planned outcome is the Evaluation step.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 95, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A nurse repositions a patient who complains of difficulty breathing. This is which phase of the nursing process?', '["Implementation", "Evaluation", "Outcome identification", "Assessment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Repositioning the patient to ease breathing is carrying out a nursing action: implementation.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 96, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'A nurse identifies the following: "Ineffective Breathing Pattern," "Anxiety," and "Deficient Knowledge regarding home medication regimen" for a postoperative client. Using Maslow''s hierarchy, which diagnosis should be given the HIGHEST priority?', '["Deficient Knowledge regarding home medication regimen", "All three are equal priority", "Anxiety", "Ineffective Breathing Pattern"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Physiological needs, including oxygenation and breathing, take precedence over psychosocial needs such as anxiety or knowledge deficits, per Maslow''s hierarchy.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 97, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Which assessment covers past medical history, family history, reason for admission, current medications, psychosocial and complete physical assessment?', '["Initial assessment", "Focused assessment", "Ongoing assessment", "Emergency assessment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The initial (admission) assessment is comprehensive: history, family history, medications, psychosocial and full physical assessment.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 98, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The following are types of advocacy except:', '["Systems Advocacy", "Self-advocacy", "Voluntary advocacy", "Individual Advocacy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The correct answer is: Systems Advocacy.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 99, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'The five steps of the nursing process, in order, are:', '["Planning, assessment, diagnosis, evaluation, implementation", "Assessment, diagnosis, planning, implementation, evaluation", "Diagnosis, assessment, planning, implementation, evaluation", "Implementation, planning, assessment, diagnosis, evaluation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The nursing process (ADPIE) follows the sequence: Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, and Evaluation, providing a systematic framework for individualised patient care.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(2, 100, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Afternoon (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues', 'Nursing diagnosis is all of these EXCEPT one', '["Uses holistic model approach", "Used for assessment of individuals and families", "Oriented to the individual, family, and community", "About actual and potential health problems"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correct answer is: Used for assessment of individuals and families.', 'NNP 425 · Advanced Nursing Concepts & Contemporary Issues'),
(3, 1, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The abbreviation FIFO in store management means:', '["Fast Inventory Follow-up Operation", "Final Item First Out", "First In, First Out", "First Into Final Order"]'::jsonb, 2, 'FIFO stands for First In, First Out, a stock rotation method.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 2, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Equipment management in ICU includes:', '["Planned maintenance, inventory and staff training", "Repairing equipment only when it fails in use", "Keeping faulty equipment at the bedside for spares", "Allowing untrained staff to set up ventilators"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each device should have a service record and user competency.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 3, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Respect for autonomy is best shown in ICU by:', '["Following the family''s wishes over the patient''s", "Making all the decisions for the patient", "Withholding information to avoid worry", "Involving competent patients in decisions"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Information is given in a way the patient understands.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 4, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The leadership style most suitable in an emergency in theatre is:', '["Absent leadership", "Laissez-faire", "Autocratic (directive)", "Democratic with long discussion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In emergencies quick decisions are needed; democratic styles suit routine planning.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 5, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which NANDA-I diagnosis is most appropriate for a patient who is unconscious with an absent gag reflex and is receiving enteral tube feedings, placing them at risk of food or fluid entering the airway?', '["Ineffective Airway Clearance", "Impaired Swallowing", "Risk for Suffocation", "Risk for Aspiration"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Risk for Aspiration describes vulnerability for gastrointestinal secretions, oropharyngeal secretions, or solids/fluids entering the tracheobronchial passages, which fits an unconscious patient with an absent gag reflex receiving tube feeds.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 6, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'An ICU nurse who leaves her shift without handover may be liable for:', '["Good professional practice", "Res ipsa loquitur", "Vicarious immunity", "Abandonment of patients"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Patient care must be handed over before leaving.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 7, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'An ICU team designs a new sedation-weaning protocol, trials it on one shift for a week, then reviews compliance and outcome data before deciding whether to adopt it unit-wide. This is an example of:', '["A root cause analysis of an incident", "A PDSA quality improvement cycle", "A retrospective nursing audit", "A risk financing strategy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Small-scale trial, data review, and decision on wider adoption is the hallmark of the Plan-Do-Study-Act improvement cycle.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 8, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which management tool helps identify a unit''s strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats?', '["Fishbone diagram", "Gantt chart", "SWOT analysis", "Flowchart"]'::jsonb, 2, 'SWOT analysis identifies an organisation''s strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 9, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which level of management is responsible for implementing programs that are intended to carry out the broader objectives of an organization set by executives?', '["chief financial managers", "supervisory managers", "middle managers", "first level managers"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Middle managers carry out programmes to achieve the broad objectives set by top executives.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 10, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which of the following is a commonly recognized high-risk category for nurses in clinical practice?', '["Medication administration errors", "Taking an approved lunch break", "Correctly documenting a normal vital sign", "Attending a staff meeting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Medication errors are one of the most frequently cited high-risk categories in nursing practice.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 11, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The unit in which comprehensive care of a critically ill patient which is deemed to recoverable stage is carried out.', '["Cardiac ICU", "Coronary Care Unit", "Critical Care Unit", "Critical Care"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A dedicated Critical Care Unit provides comprehensive care for critically ill but recoverable patients.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 12, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Hearing is thought to be the last sense lost in unconscious patients, so nurses should:', '["Talk to the patient and explain procedures", "Discuss the prognosis loudly at the bedside", "Play loud music continuously", "Avoid speaking near the bed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This respects dignity and may reduce distress.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 13, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which document outlines the duties and responsibilities of a staff nurse?', '["Ward report", "Duty roster", "Nursing care plan", "Job description"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A job description formally outlines the duties, responsibilities, and expectations of a role.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 14, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which staffing pattern provides continuous nursing care by the same nurse throughout the patient''s hospital stay?', '["Case method", "Primary nursing", "Functional nursing", "Team nursing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary nursing assigns one nurse to provide continuous, comprehensive care to a patient throughout their stay.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 15, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The rate of catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTI) in an ICU over a quarter is an example of a(n):', '["Input indicator", "Process indicator", "Outcome indicator", "Structure indicator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Outcome indicators measure the end results of care, such as complication or infection rates.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 16, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Family presence during resuscitation, when supported by a staff member, has been shown to:', '["It lengthens the resuscitation time considerably", "It increases legal claims in every case", "Helps relatives cope, without disrupting care", "It always disrupts the resuscitation team''s work"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A designated staff member should stay with the family.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 17, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Which management principle promotes employee participation in decision-making?', '["Centralization", "Unity of command", "Discipline", "Decentralization"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Decentralisation distributes decision-making authority, promoting employee participation.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 18, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Transformational leadership in ICU is characterised by:', '["Focusing only on tasks, targets and rewards", "Inspiring a shared vision and encouraging growth", "Strict rules with punishment for every mistake", "Leaving staff to make all decisions on their own"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It improves staff satisfaction and patient outcomes.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 19, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Advance directives made by a competent patient should be:', '["Followed only if the doctor agrees", "Respected when valid and applicable", "Ignored once the patient is unconscious", "Overruled by the relatives'' wishes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'They express the patient''s autonomy for future care.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 20, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The process of attracting qualified persons to apply for jobs that are open is referred to as:', '["Recruitment", "Training", "Human resource management", "Transfer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Recruitment is attracting qualified people to apply for vacant jobs. Selection then picks the best applicant.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 21, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'A patient experiences a medication error with no resulting harm. This event is classified as a:', '["Adverse event", "Near miss", "Sentinel event", "Never event"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An error that reaches the patient but causes no harm is classified as a near miss.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 22, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'A ward sister reviews a currently admitted patient''s chart mid-treatment to check whether nursing care so far meets set standards, allowing immediate correction if needed. This exemplifies:', '["Retrospective audit", "Concurrent audit", "Risk transfer", "Sentinel event review"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reviewing an active patient''s ongoing care to enable real-time correction is the hallmark of concurrent audit.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 23, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Span of control refers to:', '["The number of staff a manager directly supervises", "The total size of the hospital and its departments", "The total number of working hours in a week", "The number of operating theatres in the unit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A wide span may reduce supervision quality.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 24, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Conflict arising from unclear or overlapping job expectations between two staff members is known as:', '["Intrapersonal conflict", "Distributive conflict", "Role conflict", "Intragroup conflict"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When it''s unclear who is responsible for what, that ambiguity itself becomes a source of conflict.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 25, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'As a critical care nurse working in ICU, you know that the MOST important equipment needed for patient care is:', '["Defibrillator", "Mechanical ventilator", "Arterial blood gases analyser", "Bed side cardiac monitor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A mechanical ventilator directly sustains a critically ill patient''s life-supporting respiratory function — the most essential ICU equipment.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 26, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Telephone orders in an emergency should be:', '["Accepted without writing anything down", "Read back, documented and countersigned", "Accepted from any caller on the phone", "Ignored until a doctor attends in person"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read-back reduces errors.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 27, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'Eye care in the unconscious patient prevents:', '["Acute angle-closure glaucoma", "Retinal detachment and blindness", "Corneal ulceration from exposure", "Cataract formation in the lens"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Lubricants, moist gauze or taping the eyelids closed are used.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 28, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'The APACHE II score is used in ICU to:', '["Estimate illness severity and predicted mortality", "Calculate nursing workload for the duty roster", "Assess the depth of sedation in ventilated patients", "Measure pain in patients who cannot self-report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It uses physiological values in the first 24 hours, age and chronic health.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 29, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'A Do-Not-Resuscitate decision should be:', '["Clearly documented, discussed and reviewed", "Made by the bedside nurse alone at night", "Kept secret from the rest of the team", "Applied automatically to all elderly patients"]'::jsonb, 0, 'It applies only to CPR, not other treatments.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 30, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management', 'A nursing manager is using a concurrent audit to identify potential issues with patient care. The advantage of a concurrent audit is that it allows for:', '["A historical analysis of care over a long period", "The collection of a large amount of data from many patients at once", "Immediate intervention to correct a problem", "A review of care after the patient has been discharged"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Since a concurrent audit is performed while the patient is still receiving care, it provides an opportunity to identify and address issues in real time, preventing a poor outcome or improving the quality of care on the spot.', 'NNP 424 · Principles of Teaching and Management'),
(3, 31, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The ECG change typical of myocardial ischaemia is:', '["Widened QRS complex", "Inverted U wave", "ST-segment depression", "Appearance of Q waves"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Myocardial ischaemia typically causes ST-segment depression or T-wave inversion. Pathological Q waves indicate established infarction.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 32, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Electrocariaogram strip of a patient shows: P wave and QRS complexes --regular, PR-Interval - 0.14 seconds, QRS complexes measure- 0.08 seconds, overall hert beat rate- 82beats per minutes. This cardiac rhythm is____________', '["Sinus tachycardia", "Sinus bradycardia", "First-degree heart block", "Normal sinus rhythm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Normal PR (0.14s), normal QRS (0.08s), and a rate of 82 with regular P/QRS = normal sinus rhythm.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 33, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'In the state of shock, the intracellular environment becomes;', '["Both a & b", "Acidic", "Basic", "Alkaline"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anaerobic metabolism during shock produces lactic acid, making cells acidic.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 34, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A 2-year old was admitted for digoxin toxicity. BP is 94/60 mmhg, capillary refill time is 2 seconds and the ECG reveals 1st degree AV block with a heart rate of 60. The critical care nurse should:', '["Administer vasovagal maneuvers.", "Continue to monitor.", "Administer atropine.", "Prepare for cardioversion."]'::jsonb, 1, 'BP and cap refill are fine and 1st-degree block alone is benign — the correct action is simply to keep monitoring, not intervene.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 35, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The critical care nurse knows simultaneous injury of different regions of the body of which one or the combination of these injuries is life threatening, is popularly known as:', '["Polytrauma", "Poly head injuries", "Very severe trauma", "Severe multiple injuries"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Simultaneous life-threatening injuries across multiple body regions is the definition of polytrauma.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 36, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The transducer of an external ventricular drain is zeroed at the level of the:', '["Sternal notch", "Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space", "Iliac crest", "Tragus of the ear (foramen of Monro)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tragus approximates the foramen of Monro. Re-level after every position change.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 37, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'In the ABCDE primary survey, ''D'' stands for:', '["Documentation: write the complete history", "Diagnosis: reach a definitive diagnosis first", "Disability: GCS or AVPU, pupils and glucose", "Drugs: give analgesia before assessing pain"]'::jsonb, 2, '''E'' is exposure with prevention of hypothermia.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 38, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A mass that travels through the blood vessels (veins or arteries) and lodges elsewhere is called:', '["A thrombus", "A thromboembolin", "An embolus", "An amniotic embolism"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An embolus is any mass (clot, fat, air, amniotic fluid) carried in the bloodstream that lodges in a vessel. A thrombus is a clot fixed at the site where it formed.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 39, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'What type of anaesthesia is usually used for chest tube insertion?', '["No anaesthesia", "General anaesthesia", "Spinal anaesthesia", "Local anaesthesia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Chest drains are usually inserted under local anaesthetic infiltration, with analgesia or sedation as needed.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 40, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which of the following is NOT an indication of Central lines:', '["To monitor Blood Pressure", "Measurement of central venous pressure", "Fluid resuscitation", "Parenteral feeding"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Blood pressure is monitored via an arterial line, not the primary purpose of a central line.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 41, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which of the following is not potentially life-threatening injuries', '["Pulmonary contusion", "Aortic injuries", "Tracheobronchial injuries", "Hollow viscus injury"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Aortic injury is actually classified as an immediately (not just potentially) life-threatening injury given its rapid lethality.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 42, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO₂) below 60% suggests:', '["Excess oxygen delivery to the tissues", "Inadequate oxygen delivery for demand", "Hyperoxia from high FiO₂", "Normal tissue oxygen extraction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Normal SvO₂ is 60–80% (ScvO₂ slightly higher).', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 43, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which is NOT a cause of a high airway pressure alarm?', '["Patient biting on the tube", "Disconnected ventilator circuit", "Secretions blocking the bronchi or tube", "Attempts to speak/cough"]'::jsonb, 1, 'High airway pressure comes from coughing or attempts to speak, biting the tube, secretions, bronchospasm or pneumothorax. A disconnected circuit causes a LOW pressure alarm.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 44, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A client with acute asthma showing inspiratory and expiratory wheezes and a decreased expiratory volume should be treated with which of the following classes of medication right away?', '["Bronchodilators", "Beta-adrenergic blockers", "Inhaled steroids", "Oral steroids"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Acute bronchospasm is treated immediately with bronchodilators (inhaled β2-agonists).', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 45, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The negative inspiratory force required for weaning readiness is at least:', '["-5cmH2O", "-20cmH2O", "Not relevant to weaning", "-100cmH2O"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This measure of inspiratory muscle strength needs to reach at least this level to suggest adequate strength for weaning.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 46, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The order for putting on (donning) PPE is usually:', '["Gown, mask, goggles, gloves", "Mask, gloves, gown, goggles", "Goggles, gloves, gown, mask", "Gloves, goggles, mask, gown"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Doffing is gloves, goggles, gown, mask, then hand hygiene.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 47, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The 3 phases of Autogenic Drainage are:', '["Inhale, Pause, Exhale", "Breath Control, Expansion, Forced Expiration", "Percuss, Shake, Suction", "Unstick, Collect, Evacuate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This technique moves through low, mid, then high lung volumes to progressively move secretions upward, without needing any equipment.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 48, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A patient with a large burn is being monitored for infection. The nurse should be aware that the primary source of infection in a burn patient is:', '["The burn wound itself", "The patient''s visitors", "The patient''s own skin flora", "The hospital environment"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The burn wound itself is a perfect medium for bacterial growth. It is a large, open area of tissue that is susceptible to colonization by bacteria. The nurse must use strict aseptic technique and proper wound care to prevent infection.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 49, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Exercise has which of the following effects on clients with asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema?', '["It worsens respiratory function and is discouraged.", "It improves respiratory muscle strength.", "It reduces the number of acute attacks.", "It enhances cardiovascular fitness."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Regular exercise improves cardiovascular fitness and exercise tolerance in chronic lung disease, as in pulmonary rehabilitation.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 50, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which finding distinguishes ARDS (shock lung) from cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?', '["Raised wedge pressure above 25 mmHg", "Rapid improvement with furosemide alone", "Unilateral infiltrates with a large heart shadow", "Normal wedge pressure with bilateral infiltrates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In ARDS the left atrial pressure is not the cause; PAWP is usually 18 mmHg or less, and hypoxaemia is refractory to oxygen.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 51, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A GCS below what value is generally used as an indication for intubation?', '["<8", "<12", "<10", "<5"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A GCS under 8 suggests the patient can no longer reliably protect their own airway, so intubation is considered.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 52, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Contaminated linen from an infected patient should be:', '["Placed in a labelled, water-soluble or red bag and not shaken", "Shaken out in the corridor and then sent with the normal laundry", "Mixed with clean linen", "Washed by hand on the ward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Shaking releases organisms into the air.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 53, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A client appears upset and tearful because of pain, however refuse to take medication because he says "I do not know anything about painkillers." What is the priority intervention for this client?', '["Explain that addiction is unlikely among acute care clients", "Encourage expression of fears on past experiences", "Provide accurate information about use of pain medication", "Seek family assistance in resolving this problem"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Her fear stems from a knowledge gap about painkillers, so accurate information directly addresses the cause.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 54, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Fat provides how many kcal per gram?', '["4", "2", "7", "9"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Carbohydrate and protein provide 4 kcal/g; alcohol 7 kcal/g.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 55, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A farmer presents with goitre and reports eating cassava regularly. Which mechanism explains his condition?', '["Cassava reduces TRH secretion", "Cassava enhances thyroglobulin coupling", "Cassava inhibits iodine trapping", "Cassava increases TSH secretion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cassava contains cyanogenic compounds that interfere with the thyroid''s ability to trap iodine.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 56, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'What is the name given to mechanical contraction of the heart?', '["Depolarization.", "Diastole", "Repolarization", "Systole"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Systole is the mechanical contraction phase of the heart.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 57, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Antibiotic stewardship refers to:', '["Selecting the right drug, dose, and duration based on local resistance data", "Avoiding antibiotics entirely", "Prescribing broad-spectrum antibiotics for all infections regardless of type", "Using the maximum possible dose for every infection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Stewardship is about using antibiotics thoughtfully and appropriately to maximise effectiveness while minimising resistance development.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 58, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which condition is NOT a common medical indication for mechanical ventilation?', '["Acute allergic reaction", "Pneumonia", "Surgical procedures", "Acute respiratory distress syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An acute allergic reaction is not a typical primary indication for mechanical ventilation, unlike pneumonia, ARDS, and surgery.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 59, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which of the following types of asthma involves an acute asthma attack brought on by an upper respiratory infection?', '["Extrinsic", "Emotional", "Meditated", "Intrinsic"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Intrinsic (non-allergic) asthma is triggered by infection, exercise, cold and similar factors. Extrinsic asthma is allergic.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 60, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The mode of Mechanical Ventilation which does not allow the patient to take spontaneous breaths is:', '["PCV (Pressure Control Mode)", "CMV (Control Mandatory Ventilation)", "SIMV (Synchronous intermittent Mandatory Ventilation)", "AC Mode (Assist Control Mode)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Control Mandatory Ventilation delivers only preset breaths with no room for spontaneous effort.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 61, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A patient with a high spinal cord injury develops hypotension WITH bradycardia (rather than the expected compensatory tachycardia). This is most consistent with:', '["Hypovolaemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Septic shock", "Neurogenic shock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Neurogenic shock, from disrupted sympathetic tone after high spinal cord injury, is distinguished by hypotension with bradycardia rather than the tachycardia seen in most other shock types.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 62, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Head elevation of 30–45° is recommended in inhalational injury management to:', '["Prevent pressure sores", "Improve appetite", "Reduce airway oedema", "Improve hearing"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Elevating the head helps reduce swelling around the airway, lowering the risk of obstruction.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 63, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Chlorhexidine has an advantage over povidone-iodine for skin prep because it:', '["Is not flammable when mixed with alcohol", "Works only on wet skin", "Is safe for the eyes and middle ear", "Has a longer residual effect on the skin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Alcohol-based chlorhexidine is flammable and must dry.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 64, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The monophasic shock energy is used:', '["150-200 J", "120-200 J", "200-300 J", "100-200 J"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Monophasic defibrillators typically need higher energy, commonly cited around 200-300J (often fixed at 360J).', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 65, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which nursing intervention is appropriate to prevent pulmonary embolus in a patient who is prescribed bed rest?', '["Encourage deep breathing and coughing", "Use the knee gatch when the client is in bed", "Limit the client''s fluid intake", "Teach the patient to move legs in bed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Leg movement/exercises promote venous return and reduce clot formation, directly targeting DVT/PE prevention.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 66, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Postural drainage positions are based specifically on:', '["Random rotation regardless of anatomy", "Bronchopulmonary segmental anatomy", "Blood pressure readings", "Patient preference alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each of the 12 positions is designed to match a specific lung segment''s anatomical drainage pathway.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 67, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The commonest thrombolytic agent used to establish reperfusion if there are no contraindications to its use in a patient with myocardial infarction is:', '["Pethidine", "Aspirin", "Morphine sulphate", "Streptokinase"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Streptokinase is the classic, widely used thrombolytic for reperfusion in MI.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 68, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A patient is inhaling 500 ml of air with 75% Fio2, which of the following term the nurse should document for the amount of air inhaled?', '["tidal volume", "residual volume", "total lung capacity", "minute volume"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The volume of one single breath is tidal volume.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 69, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A 20 year male presented to ER with motor bike accident. The Nurse is performing initial assessment of the patient in triage and finds out that the patient has delayed capillary refill, Cool, Moist skin, Decreasing blood pressure and increasing heart rate. What is the suspected medical diagnose of this patient?', '["Hypotension", "Shock", "Hemorrhage", "Acute Renal Failure(ARF)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cool moist skin, delayed cap refill, falling BP, rising HR together describe shock.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 70, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'On a pulmonary artery catheter, which reading confirms pulmonary arterial hypertension rather than left heart disease?', '["Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg", "Mean PAP 18 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 8 mmHg", "Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 28 mmHg", "Mean PAP 12 mmHg with a high cardiac output and wedge 6"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pre-capillary PH has a raised mean PAP (over 20–25 mmHg) with a normal wedge (15 mmHg or less). A high wedge points to left heart disease.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 71, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Intra-abdominal hypertension grade III corresponds to an IAP of:', '["Over 25 mmHg", "12–15 mmHg", "16–20 mmHg", "21–25 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Grade I is 12–15, grade II 16–20, grade III 21–25 and grade IV over 25 mmHg.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 72, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which viral hepatitis is the most common viral cause of fulminant hepatic failure?', '["Hepatitis A", "Hepatitis B", "Hepatitis E only", "Hepatitis C"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Among the viral causes, hepatitis B is the most frequently implicated in fulminant liver failure.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 73, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Artificial trans-fat is a type of fat that is added to some food products to improve taste, texture and extend their shelf life. However, trans fats can increase your risk of ............', '["Lung diseases", "Diabetes", "Kidney diseases", "Heart diseases"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Trans fats are strongly linked to increased heart disease risk.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 74, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A nurse is assessing a patient for signs of respiratory failure. Which of the following is an early sign of hypoxemia?', '["Bradycardia", "Restlessness and confusion", "Cyanosis", "A decrease in respiratory rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Early signs of hypoxemia are often neurological, as the brain is very sensitive to a lack of oxygen. The patient may appear restless, confused, or anxious. Cyanosis is a late sign of hypoxemia, and bradycardia is a very late sign.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 75, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Hemodynamic monitoring consist of;', '["None of above", "Invasive", "Non invasive", "Both of them"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hemodynamic monitoring includes both invasive (arterial/CVP/PAC) and non-invasive (BP cuff, ECG) methods.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 76, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A trauma patient on beta-blockers loses 1.5 L of blood. Why may the class of haemorrhage be underestimated?', '["They raise the BP and so hide hypotension", "They blunt the tachycardic response", "They cause a diuresis that masks oliguria", "They keep the patient alert despite shock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Elderly patients, athletes and those on beta-blockers or pacemakers may not show tachycardia.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 77, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', '2 sec what does it indiciates?', '["third degree block", "first degree block", "second degree type-I block", "second degree type-II block"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A pause of this length in the described tracing best fits complete (third-degree) block; question stem is incomplete, answered on best available context.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 78, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A nurse monitors a thyroidectomy patient for thyroid crisis. The most important vital sign to observe is:', '["Blood pressure", "Temperature", "Respiratory rate", "Pulse rate"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pulse rate is the most immediately telling vital sign for detecting the tachycardia of an evolving thyroid crisis.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 79, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Professional nurses are responsible for making clinical decisions to', '["Apply clear textbook solutions to patients'' problems.", "Take immediate action when a patient''s condition worsens.", "Formulate standardized care plans for groups of patients", "Prove traditional methods of providing nursing care to patients."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taking prompt clinical action as a patient''s condition changes reflects professional clinical decision-making.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 80, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The balloon of IABP is positioned in the descending thoracic aorta 1 to 2 cm below:', '["Left Subclavian artery", "Right Subclavian Artery", "Coronary arteries", "Renal artery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The balloon sits 1-2cm below the origin of the left subclavian artery.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 81, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'This denotes a disease of the alveoli characterized by irreversible damage to the alveoli making them less elastic, leading to decrease exchange of gasses causing hypoxemia.', '["Pulmonary Emphysema", "Pulmonary Congestion", "Pulmonary hypertension", "Pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Irreversible alveolar damage reducing elasticity and gas exchange describes pulmonary emphysema.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 82, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The nurse is administering oxygen to a patient with a history of COPD. The nurse should use caution with high-flow oxygen because it can:', '["Lead to hypoxemia", "Cause a sudden increase in the patient''s respiratory rate", "Cause a decrease in the patient''s blood pressure", "Lead to a decrease in the patient''s respiratory drive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In some patients with COPD, the primary stimulus to breathe is a low PaO₂. Administering high-flow oxygen can remove this stimulus, leading to a decrease in the patient''s respiratory drive and hypoventilation.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 83, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Neurogenic shock is distinguished from other types of shock by:', '["Always presenting with fever", "Tachycardia", "Bradycardia", "No change in heart rate"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Unlike most shock states which trigger a compensatory tachycardia, neurogenic shock characteristically causes bradycardia due to loss of sympathetic tone.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 84, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A patient requires a low, consistent concentration of oxygen. The most appropriate oxygen delivery device for this purpose is a:', '["Venturi mask", "Non-rebreather mask", "Nasal cannula", "Simple face mask"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A Venturi mask is a high-flow system that delivers a precise and consistent concentration of oxygen, regardless of the patient''s breathing pattern. The FiO₂ can be adjusted by changing the adapter.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 85, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'What can cause critical obstruction in a child''s airway due to narrow anatomy?', '["Excessive adenoid dislodgement", "Retrograde escape of gas", "Swelling of the glottis", "Large tracheal tube"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Glottic swelling can critically narrow a child''s already small airway.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 86, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'High frequency ventilation has the advantage of delivering ........ and reducing the incidence of .........', '["Large mean airway pressure; necrotizing tracheobronchitis", "Low peak inspiratory pressure; air trapping", "Large tidal volume; barotraumas", "Small tidal volume; barotraumas"]'::jsonb, 3, 'High-frequency ventilation delivers very small tidal volumes, reducing barotrauma risk.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 87, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A nurse is monitoring a patient''s oxygen saturation with a pulse oximeter. A reading of 92% is considered:', '["Normal", "Mild hypoxemia", "Severe hypoxemia", "A sign of hyperoxia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A normal oxygen saturation is typically 95% or higher. A reading of 92% is considered mild hypoxemia and may require an increase in oxygen therapy or further assessment.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 88, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A nurse is providing wound care for a patient with a burn. The main goal of burn wound care is to:', '["Prevent infection and promote healing", "Keep the wound wet to prevent infection", "Only use topical antibiotics", "Promote the formation of eschar"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The primary goals of burn wound care are to prevent infection, which is a major complication, and to create an optimal environment for wound healing. This often involves cleaning the wound, debriding dead tissue, and applying dressings with or without topical antimicrobial agents.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 89, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The normal mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) is approximately:', '["70–75%", "40–45%", "50–55%", "95–100%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mixed venous blood normally has PvO2 about 40 mmHg and SvO2 about 70–75%.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 90, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which is NOT a contraindication to chest tube insertion?', '["Uncontrolled bleeding disorder", "Refusal by a competent patient", "Suspected tension pneumothorax", "Skin infection at the insertion site"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Tension pneumothorax is an INDICATION (after needle decompression) for a chest drain. Coagulopathy and skin infection at the site are relative contraindications.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 91, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Chloride shift is also known as _______', '["Sodium concentration phenomenon", "Hamburger phenomenon", "Chloride phenomenon", "Cardio phenomenon"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Chloride shift is also called the Hamburger phenomenon.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 92, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which of the following is the initial analgesic drug of choice for a patient who is in severe pain, with a score of 9 out of 10, due to a fracture?', '["Rectal NSAID", "IV opiate", "Splint", "IV paracetamol"]'::jsonb, 1, 'IV opioid is the appropriate first-line choice for severe (9/10) acute fracture pain.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 93, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The correct compression-to-ventilation ratio for a single rescuer performing adult CPR is:', '["5:1", "15:1", "30:2", "15:2"]'::jsonb, 2, 'For a single rescuer on an adult, 30 compressions are followed by 2 breaths, repeated in cycles.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 94, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Risk factor modification is an important component of patient education and rehabilitation after myocardial infarction. The critical care nurse knows one of the following as a major controlled risk factor:', '["Excessive alcohol intake", "Stress", "Obesity", "Age"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Obesity is a modifiable ("controllable") risk factor for MI, unlike age.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 95, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A 19-year-old comes into the emergency department with acute asthma. His respiratory rate is 44 breaths/minute, and he appears to be in acute respiratory distress. Which of the following actions should be taken FIRST?', '["Apply a cardiac monitor to the client.", "Provide emotional support to the client.", "Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.", "Take a full medication history."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In acute severe asthma, give a nebulised bronchodilator first. History can wait.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 96, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A burn wound produces green-blue pus with a sweet, grape-like smell. The most likely organism is:', '["Clostridium perfringens", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Candida albicans", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pseudomonas produces blue-green pigments (pyocyanin) and is common in burns, moist environments and ventilated patients. It is often resistant to many antibiotics.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 97, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'The hepatitis B vaccination course for health workers is usually given at:', '["A single dose at birth only", "0, 1, 2 and 3 years", "0, 1 and 6 months", "0 and 12 months only"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Three doses at 0, 1 and 6 months (an accelerated schedule exists), followed by a check of anti-HBs antibody level 1–2 months after completion to confirm immunity.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 98, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A patient with an opening in the chest wall (e.g. from a gunshot or stab wound) resulting in a "sucking chest wound" has:', '["An open pneumothorax", "A closed pneumothorax", "A pleural effusion", "A haemothorax"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A penetrating chest wall defect that lets air move in and out ("sucking chest wound") is an open pneumothorax. Cover it with a three-sided occlusive dressing.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 99, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'Which of the following Unit is used for the measurement of Cardiac Index?', '["L/Min/m2", "L/m2/Min", "Min/L/m2", "m/Min/L2"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cardiac index is expressed in L/min/m².', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)'),
(3, 100, 'Nephrology Nursing Mock — Day 4 Night (Nov 2026)', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)', 'A family member asks you, "Why can''t you give more medicine? He is still having a lot of pain." What is your best response?', '["\"Let''s wait about 30-40 minutes. If there is no relief I''ll call the doctor.\"", "\"If the medication is given too frequently he could suffer ill effects.\"", "\"Please tell him that I will be right there to check of him.\"", "\"The doctor ordered the medicine to be given every 4 hours.\""]'::jsonb, 1, 'Explaining the safety rationale (risk of harm from over-frequent dosing) is honest and reassuring without overpromising.', 'Comprehensive Revision (Critical Care, Emergencies & Infection Control)');
